@@ -795,3 +796,46 @@
 
 The gateway's `TestMain` gives the package a home of its own, so these tests
 never read real providers or accounts.
+
+
+## Response completion deadlines
+
+`settings.json` fields `responseIdle` and `responseTotal` are durations in
+seconds. Zero chooses the defaults, 300 and 1800 seconds; negative values
+and values that overflow a Go duration are rejected on save and when a
+request reads the settings. They are independent of a group's FirstToken.
+
+`responseWaiting` in [`response_wait.go`](../../internal/gateway/response_wait.go)
+starts the total budget before `serve` plans candidates or enters their
+queues. A local `codexCompact` starts it outside both summary asks, so a
+plain retry, fallback, retry pause or replan inherits the remaining budget.
+Native compaction still follows its original passthrough and seal ownership
+checks. The deadline is not an agent identity or checkpoint source.
+
+The idle clock starts when an upstream response has headers. Non-whitespace
+JSON body bytes, or complete SSE events that the existing protocol parser
+recognizes as content, reasoning, tool progress or a terminal event, count
+as progress. Comments, pings, empty deltas and framing alone do not. The
+observer preserves bytes and framing, keeps at most the existing 32 MiB
+scanner limit per observed event, and treats a larger event as unknown until
+the next boundary. A valid terminal event ends reading even if the peer
+leaves the connection open. Completion and late progress are judged by the
+monotonic observation time, not by when a timer goroutine is scheduled.
+
+On idle expiry an unsent attempt may move only to a remaining eligible
+candidate. It never retries that candidate or rests its account for this
+local deadline. Total expiry stops the route, including a retry pause or
+queue. Both cancel the upstream request and close its body. Held JSON and
+local summaries are not released partially. A local timeout in a second
+summary ask cannot become a heuristic local summary; ordinary non-timeout
+fallbacks retain their prior behavior.
+
+Before headers are sent, the error is HTTP 504 with `response_idle` or
+`response_total`. An already-started SSE stream retains its HTTP status and
+gets the existing protocol error event. A committed non-stream response is
+never given SSE text. An upstream's own 504 and a caller's cancellation
+(499) remain distinct; a local timeout is not recorded as the agent leaving.
+
+Verified by `TestResponseWait*` (local synthetic HTTP upstreams) and
+`TestResponseObserver*` / settings `TestResponseWaitSettings` (protocol,
+monotonic boundary, bounded buffering and configuration checks).
